(a) The records management officer shall act for the county records committee in matters of:
(1) Establishing and maintaining an active and continuing program for the economical, efficient and effective management of records;
(2) Developing and issuing rules, procedures and regulations subject to review by the county records committee, the department of archives and history, and the state records committee;
(3) Establishing standards for economical, efficient management relating to the creation, utilization, maintenance, retention, preservation and disposition of records, filing and copying equipment, supplies, microfilming of records, and vital records programs;
(4) Assisting the head of each county administrative office and the office of each county elected public official in developing and implementing a program of records management;
(5) Implementing records management procedures and regulations approved by the county records committee;
(6) Developing a records management center program.
(b) It shall be the duty of the records management officer to:
(1) Direct the records management program to achieve efficient and economical management practices relating to the creation, maintenance, utilization, retention, preservation and disposition of records, filing equipment, microfilm equipment, supplies, microfilming of records and vital records;
(2) Assist in the scheduling of records, develop a records control schedule for the county and maintain the schedule on a current basis;
(3) Supervise implementation of records retention schedule;
(4) Supervise physical transfer of records from high-cost office space to low-cost records center or an archival depository;
(5) Conduct a microfilm program and supervise a county microfilm laboratory;
(6) Develop and supervise a program for the training of records management assistants and filing personnel;
(7) Prepare and submit reports of status and progress of the records management program to the county records committee;
(8) Review, evaluate and recommend more efficient paperwork that will simplify operations throughout all offices of the county;
(9) Conduct surveys to determine the most efficient and economical filing systems and filing equipment for use by the county;
(10) Recommend the selection and utilization of filing equipment and supplies.
(Res. of 7-12-88)

34:
It shall be the duty of the head of each administrative office of the county to:
(1) Cooperate fully with the county records committee and the records management officer in complying with the provisions of this article and the provisions of the state records act;
(2) Cause to be made and preserve records containing adequate and proper documentation of the organization policies, decisions, procedures and essential transactions of the office and designed to furnish the information necessary to protect the legal and financial rights of the county and of persons directly affected by the office's activities;
(3) Implement records management procedures and regulations issued by the county records committee and the records management officer in conjunction with the department of archives and history and the state records committee;
(4) Submit to the records management officer, in accordance with the rules and regulations of the state records committee, a recommended disposition standard for each records series in his custody;
(5) Establish necessary safeguards against the removal or loss of records and such further safeguards as may be recommended by the records management officer. Such safeguards shall include notification of all officials and personnel that county records are public property and as such shall constitute a record of public acts. Further, that such records shall not be placed in the custody of private or semipublic institutions or turned over to private individuals, and that no records in the custody of a county office are to be destroyed or otherwise alienated except in accordance with the provisions of the state records act.
(Res. of 7-12-88)

